{
 "language_code": "eng",
 "language_probability": 0.9891999959945679,
 "text": "This title begins with the Chinese word for geometry. It is often said to copy the sound of geo. In 1606, two men in Beijing began to translate Euclid's geometry. Only one of them could read Latin. Chinese mathematics was written as questions, such as how large a field 15 paces by 16 is. That question ends with the title's first two characters. A commentary written in 263 CE justified those methods by cutting figures apart and reassembling them. Euclid never asks a question, and he defines every object first. Chinese had no word for definition, so Ricci and Xu made one meaning, an account of boundaries. A, B, and C were only sounds, so they used a series every literate reader could recite. Those two characters were the ordinary word for how much. Ricci and Xu used them to mean quantity. For 250 years, Chinese readers had only six of Euclid's books, all on plane geometry, so the word for how much came to mean geometry.",
